WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.387
Welcome to Module 1, Understanding Data in Informatics.

00:00:04.387 --> 00:00:09.317
This module is the foundation for the rest of IAN 603.

00:00:09.317 --> 00:00:14.042
Before we clean, transform, query, or model data, we need to understand what the data represents,

00:00:14.042 --> 00:00:21.872
how it is organized, and whether it is trustworthy enough for the task.

00:00:21.872 --> 00:00:26.897
We will use small examples and one running patient-encounter dataset so the ideas remain concrete.

00:00:26.897 --> 00:00:29.472
The goal is not to memorize vocabulary.

00:00:29.472 --> 00:00:36.357
The goal is to build a habit: understand the data first, then make technical decisions.

00:00:36.357 --> 00:00:44.782
Keep a real project in mind as we work through this section—for example, a hospital combining patient tables,

00:00:44.782 --> 00:00:47.992
device JSON, and clinical notes.

00:00:47.992 --> 00:00:55.072
The technical feature matters because it changes what questions we can answer reliably and how easily another

00:00:55.072 --> 00:00:58.592
analyst can understand our work.

00:00:58.592 --> 00:01:03.032
As you listen, try to connect each SQL or data concept to a decision you might need to

00:01:03.032 --> 00:01:09.292
defend later: what the data means, what one row represents, what can be missing,

00:01:09.292 --> 00:01:13.522
and what assumptions the transformation introduces.
